 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Spectral Bias:** Standard ReLU MLPs struggle to learn high-frequency details, often failing to reach sharp reconstructions even after many training steps (e.g., 2000 steps) compared to SIREN [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Coordinate Convention:** Inputs must be normalized to the $[-1, 1]$ range [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Gradient Requirement:** The input tensor must be cloned with gradients enabled to compute derivatives for optimization or rendering (e.g., for sphere tracing or volume rendering gradients) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Spectral Bias:** Standard ReLU-MLPs suffer from spectral bias, meaning they learn low-frequency functions faster than high-frequency ones. This results in blurry reconstructions compared to SIREN, which uses sine activations [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Coordinate Normalization:** Inputs should follow the $[-1, 1]$ convention to ensure stable training and gradient magnitudes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Gradient Computation:** The input coordinates must be cloned with gradients enabled to compute derivatives for rendering (e.g., for sphere tracing or volume rendering integrals) [S1, unofficial].
 
